  "response": "# Python Currency Formatting Function\n\n```python\ndef format_currency(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Format a number as currency string with $ and thousand separators.\n    \n    Args:\n        n (int or float): The number to format\n        \n    Returns:\n        str: Formatted currency string like \"$4,640,039\" or \"-$500\"\n    \"\"\"\n    # Handle negative numbers by adding minus sign before $\n    if n < 0:\n        return f\"${n}\"\n    else:\n        return f\"${n:,}\"\n```\n\n**Improved version for cleaner formatting:**\n\n```python\ndef format_currency(n):\n    \"\"\"Format a number as currency string with $ and thousand separators.\"\"\"\n    formatted = f\"{n:,}\"  # Adds commas every 3 digits\n    if n < 0:\n        return f\"-${formatted.lstrip('-')}\"\n    else:\n        return f\"${formatted}\"\n```\n\n**Simplest approach:**\n\n```python\ndef format_currency(n):\n    \"\"\"Format a number as currency string with $ and thousand separators.\"\"\"\n    return f\"-${n}\".replace(\"$\", \"\")  # This won't work correctly\n    \n# Better simple version:\ndef format_currency(n):\n    if n < 0:\n        return f\"-${-n:,.0f}\"\n    else:\n        return f\"${n:,.0f}\"\n```\n\n**Best solution:**\n\n```python\ndef format_currency(n):\n    \"\"\"Format a number as currency string with $ and thousand separators.\"\"\"\n    sign = \"-\" if n < 0 else \"\"\n    abs_n = abs(n)\n    return f\"{sign}${abs_n:,.0f}\"\n```\n\n## Usage Examples:\n\n```python\nprint(format_currency(4640039))     # $4,640,039\nprint(format_currency(-4640039))    # -$4,640,039\nprint(format_currency(12345))       # $12,345\nprint(format_currency(-100))        # -$100\nprint(format_currency(0.5))         # $.5 or you could round if needed\n```\n\nThe **best solution** handles all cases cleanly:\n- Uses `abs()` to get absolute value for formatting\n- Prepends minus sign before `$` for negative numbers\n- Uses Python's built-in comma separator in the format string",
